WEBVTT

1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,833
{\an8}["You Get What You Give"
by the New Radicals playing]

2
00:00:08,916 --> 00:00:13,500
{\an8}[man] <i>They say that childhood is</i>
<i>the best time to build new friendships.</i>

3
00:00:15,291 --> 00:00:16,708
<i>That's me: Olli.</i>

4
00:00:16,791 --> 00:00:18,041
Hey, kick it right over here!

5
00:00:18,125 --> 00:00:22,875
[Olli] <i>Eight years old and already one of</i>
<i>the coolest, most likable guys around.</i>

6
00:00:24,041 --> 00:00:26,000
<i>Problem was, no one had noticed it yet.</i>

7
00:00:26,083 --> 00:00:28,625
No wonder you always get picked last!

8
00:00:28,708 --> 00:00:31,666
[Olli] <i>But then, suddenly,</i>
<i>Matze wound up in my class.</i>

9
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:34,541
Okay, guys, let's meet
our new classmate here, Matthias.

10
00:00:34,625 --> 00:00:37,625
Tell us a little about yourself.
Do you have any hobbies?

11
00:00:37,708 --> 00:00:42,500
[stuttering] I like to play soccer.

12
00:00:42,583 --> 00:00:44,166
[students laughing]

13
00:00:44,250 --> 00:00:46,166
[teacher] Enough.
I don't see what's so funny.

14
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:47,958
Matthias, please sit over there.

15
00:00:48,041 --> 00:00:50,875
So, let's continue
with different types of sentences.

16
00:00:50,958 --> 00:00:52,250
For example…

17
00:00:52,333 --> 00:00:53,208
Uh, hey, I'm Olli.

18
00:00:53,291 --> 00:00:54,750
-[teacher] "Where is my pen?"
-M-M-Matze.

19
00:00:54,833 --> 00:00:56,250
So you play soccer, right?

20
00:00:56,333 --> 00:01:00,166
Oliver, stop bothering Matthias
or I'll have to move him to another desk.

21
00:01:00,958 --> 00:01:03,541
He-He didn't… I…
I… just asked him a question.

22
00:01:03,625 --> 00:01:07,666
He-he-he he-he didn't do anything wrong,
he-he-he was just answering.

23
00:01:08,333 --> 00:01:12,833
[Olli] <i>That was it. That moment was</i>
<i>the beginning of a beautiful friendship.</i>

24
00:01:13,416 --> 00:01:15,708
-[school bell rings]
<i>-♪ You got the music in you ♪</i>

25
00:01:15,791 --> 00:01:17,541
<i>♪ Don't let go ♪</i>

26
00:01:17,625 --> 00:01:19,750
<i>♪ You got the music in you ♪</i>

27
00:01:19,833 --> 00:01:23,916
<i>♪ 4 a.m., we ran a miracle mile ♪</i>
-[swan honking]

28
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:25,583
<i>♪ We're flat broke </i>♪

29
00:01:25,666 --> 00:01:28,250
-[skateboard wheels rolling]
-♪ <i>But hey, we do it in style ♪</i>

30
00:01:28,333 --> 00:01:33,833
<i>♪ The bad rich</i>
<i>God's flying in for your trial ♪</i>

31
00:01:33,916 --> 00:01:35,500
<i>♪ Ha-ha-ha ♪</i>

32
00:01:35,583 --> 00:01:40,375
MY BEST FRIEND, HIS GIRLFRIEND AND ME

33
00:01:40,458 --> 00:01:43,041
<i>♪ You cannot find a friend ♪</i>

34
00:01:43,125 --> 00:01:44,250
-[engine revving]
<i>-♪ Friend ♪</i>

35
00:01:44,333 --> 00:01:47,958
<i>♪ You feel your tree is breaking ♪</i>

36
00:01:48,041 --> 00:01:50,458
<i>♪ Just bend ♪</i>

37
00:01:50,541 --> 00:01:51,458
[cheering]

38
00:01:51,541 --> 00:01:53,541
<i>♪ You got the music in you ♪</i>

39
00:01:53,625 --> 00:01:55,208
<i>♪ Don't let go ♪</i>

40
00:01:55,291 --> 00:01:57,083
<i>♪ You got the music in you ♪</i>

41
00:01:57,166 --> 00:01:59,291
I was trying to be a good guy, you know?

42
00:01:59,375 --> 00:02:01,958
-[chatting continues]
<i>-♪ This world is gonna pull through ♪</i>

43
00:02:02,041 --> 00:02:04,000
<i>♪ Don't give up ♪</i>

44
00:02:04,083 --> 00:02:06,166
<i>♪ You've got a reason to live ♪</i>

45
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:09,166
[Matze] Good news.
I found the perfect apartment

46
00:02:09,250 --> 00:02:10,500
for you and your cat.

47
00:02:10,583 --> 00:02:12,625
That's right, ground floor.

48
00:02:12,708 --> 00:02:14,333
Yup, great. I'll pick you up.

49
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:17,541
Hey, come here.

50
00:02:19,166 --> 00:02:21,708
[intriguing music playing]

51
00:02:21,791 --> 00:02:23,041
[Matze] Where is that?

52
00:02:23,125 --> 00:02:25,750
Zanzibar. Island off Tanzania.

53
00:02:26,333 --> 00:02:27,875
[Matze] That has to go on the list.

54
00:02:27,958 --> 00:02:29,291
Well, that's fascinating.

55
00:02:29,375 --> 00:02:32,500
I wasn't aware that luxury bamboo huts
were part of our listing portfolio.

56
00:02:32,583 --> 00:02:33,625
[Olli clears throat]

57
00:02:34,208 --> 00:02:35,666
Supposed to be working, right?

58
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:42,583
[stuttering] Yeah, I…

59
00:02:42,666 --> 00:02:44,791
The flat on M-Molke Street, I closed it.

60
00:02:44,875 --> 00:02:45,708
[boss] Mm-hmm.

61
00:02:45,791 --> 00:02:49,000
How about you? You just been
planning your trip around the world?

62
00:02:49,791 --> 00:02:54,291
I… brought you
your favorite little special something.

63
00:02:54,375 --> 00:02:55,541
[boss] Ah.

64
00:02:57,125 --> 00:02:58,833
I don't know what's more impressive to me,

65
00:02:59,416 --> 00:03:01,208
your perfect timing,

66
00:03:01,291 --> 00:03:04,583
your ability to finish work
and get out the door right on the dot…

67
00:03:06,291 --> 00:03:08,916
Mm! Or your very clever career planning.

68
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,750
I mean, who doesn't take a year off
in their early thirties?

69
00:03:11,833 --> 00:03:14,041
By the way, the Mülheim apartment
has a scheduled viewing,

70
00:03:14,125 --> 00:03:17,708
only the person won't be there
till seven tonight. Here's the address.

71
00:03:19,375 --> 00:03:21,166
Seven? I'm off the clock.

72
00:03:21,250 --> 00:03:23,333
I know, it compensates
for the time you spent just now

73
00:03:23,416 --> 00:03:25,708
making plans for your midlife crisis.

74
00:03:25,791 --> 00:03:28,833
Oh, and you better not put this
on your expense report, Olli.

75
00:03:29,833 --> 00:03:32,208
Would it kill the Empress
to give me a compliment?

76
00:03:32,291 --> 00:03:34,166
That apartment on Molke Street
was a tough one.

77
00:03:34,958 --> 00:03:37,583
You mind… taking care of this, Matze?

78
00:03:37,666 --> 00:03:39,583
I need to run and get
Schmitti's birthday present.

79
00:03:40,208 --> 00:03:42,791
I thought we were getting Schmitti
wine from the gas station.

80
00:03:42,875 --> 00:03:44,541
I came up with something else.

81
00:03:45,750 --> 00:03:48,625
[upbeat rock music playing]

82
00:03:48,708 --> 00:03:51,291
-[Matze] This is it. Looks nice.
-[Olli] Wow, an actual adult house.

83
00:03:51,875 --> 00:03:53,250
[ringing]

84
00:03:54,416 --> 00:03:57,291
[both] Happy Birthday, Schmitti!

85
00:03:57,375 --> 00:03:59,125
Shh! Don't!

86
00:03:59,208 --> 00:04:01,625
It's been over two hours!
Everybody's done eating!

87
00:04:01,708 --> 00:04:03,875
C'mon, we're not mad, it's no big deal.

88
00:04:04,708 --> 00:04:07,291
For you. You know,
now that you're a homeowner.

89
00:04:07,375 --> 00:04:09,958
Right, thanks. Listen up, okay?
Behave yourselves.

90
00:04:10,041 --> 00:04:12,833
This isn't some rager,
I don't want a bunch of police at my door.

91
00:04:12,916 --> 00:04:14,666
Hard enough
to convince Antje to invite you.

92
00:04:14,750 --> 00:04:16,458
Really? It's your birthday.

93
00:04:16,541 --> 00:04:19,250
That was the only reason she let me.
Wait, wait, wait. Hold on.

94
00:04:21,583 --> 00:04:22,916
Put these on first, okay?

95
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
[soft music playing in background]

96
00:04:26,416 --> 00:04:29,500
It's a trendy shade of gray right now.
It's a mix of slab and granite.

97
00:04:29,583 --> 00:04:30,791
[plastic crinkling]

98
00:04:30,875 --> 00:04:31,916
Oh, don't let us interrupt.

99
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,166
I was hoping you'd be
on your world tour already.

100
00:04:34,250 --> 00:04:35,833
Not for another two months.

101
00:04:35,916 --> 00:04:36,750
Mmm.

102
00:04:37,250 --> 00:04:39,166
-Great place, Schmitti.
-[Schmitti] Thank you.

103
00:04:39,250 --> 00:04:41,375
Didn't know you made this much
in the pan department.

104
00:04:41,958 --> 00:04:45,375
Household wares. And I'm not a salesman
anymore, I'm a sales manager now.

105
00:04:45,458 --> 00:04:46,291
Wow, since when?

106
00:04:46,375 --> 00:04:48,958
-For the last one or two years.
-Schmitti, good call on the bidet.

107
00:04:49,041 --> 00:04:50,416
Yeah, yeah, but it's not quite--

108
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:51,541
Yes-- [gasps]

109
00:04:51,625 --> 00:04:53,041
-[bidet whirs]
-[water bubbling]

110
00:04:53,625 --> 00:04:55,958
[upbeat rock continues]

111
00:04:58,083 --> 00:05:00,583
[woman] The place is
beautifully decorated, Antje.

112
00:05:00,666 --> 00:05:03,458
Thank you. It was all a team effort.
Right, babe?

113
00:05:03,541 --> 00:05:05,083
Oh, yeah. Definitely, yep.

114
00:05:06,791 --> 00:05:08,916
Huh. So this is your room, Schmitti?

115
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,291
Oh my God, Antje?

116
00:05:10,375 --> 00:05:13,125
Wait, you're pregnant? [gasps] Since when?

117
00:05:13,208 --> 00:05:15,416
Not quite yet. [chuckles]

118
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:18,458
But I bet we'll be first.
You guys are all too scared.

119
00:05:18,541 --> 00:05:20,625
[Olli] Uh, not so fast there, Antje.

120
00:05:20,708 --> 00:05:23,375
[Matze] Olli is, uh,
already at seven months.

121
00:05:23,458 --> 00:05:26,875
Third trimester. I'm always so hungry.
Have to eat for two now.

122
00:05:28,333 --> 00:05:29,666
Leftovers are downstairs.

123
00:05:33,083 --> 00:05:34,166
[Matze] Mm.

124
00:05:37,750 --> 00:05:40,291
[Olli] Oh, dude! Got himself a smart home.

125
00:05:41,416 --> 00:05:42,291
[Matze] Huh.

126
00:05:45,125 --> 00:05:46,166
[Olli] Oh.

127
00:05:46,666 --> 00:05:47,541
What does this do?

128
00:05:47,625 --> 00:05:49,250
[Olli] No idea.

129
00:05:49,333 --> 00:05:50,750
[rapid beeping]

130
00:05:50,833 --> 00:05:54,916
[alarm blaring]

131
00:05:57,208 --> 00:05:58,375
The hell happened?

132
00:05:58,875 --> 00:06:00,500
Nothing, just… pressed the screen.

133
00:06:00,583 --> 00:06:02,875
-That's the alarm, you idiot!
-My bad.

134
00:06:02,958 --> 00:06:04,291
[Schmitti] Shoot…

135
00:06:04,375 --> 00:06:06,666
-Ralf, turn the alarm off already!
-I know.

136
00:06:06,750 --> 00:06:09,041
You don't know the code?
You installed this thing!

137
00:06:09,125 --> 00:06:10,916
Yeah, but it's still not set up yet.

138
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,250
Find the manual, turn it off! Go!

139
00:06:15,291 --> 00:06:16,125
[Schmitti grunts]

140
00:06:16,958 --> 00:06:18,875
Ow… Yep, all good!

141
00:06:18,958 --> 00:06:20,250
[alarm continues]

142
00:06:23,666 --> 00:06:24,541
[rings]

143
00:06:25,791 --> 00:06:28,666
Hey, honey, uh, these two were outside.

144
00:06:28,750 --> 00:06:30,583
I guess the neighbors
called the police for us.

145
00:06:30,666 --> 00:06:32,166
-Find the instructions?
-Not yet.

146
00:06:32,250 --> 00:06:34,000
-Then go get it!
-Okay.

147
00:06:35,416 --> 00:06:37,291
-[grunts]
-[annoyed exhale]

148
00:06:37,375 --> 00:06:38,500
All good, thank you.

149
00:06:39,125 --> 00:06:41,416
-[Antje] I'm really sorry about this.
-Can I give it a go?

150
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:43,416
Yes, go ahead, take a look.

151
00:06:47,291 --> 00:06:48,833
-[alarm stops]
-[sighs]

152
00:06:49,333 --> 00:06:50,208
Thank you.

153
00:06:50,291 --> 00:06:53,000
The code is 1-2-3-4,
like most factory settings.

154
00:06:53,666 --> 00:06:55,166
It reeks of cannabis.

155
00:06:56,041 --> 00:06:57,833
Pff! That's impossible, officer.

156
00:07:08,875 --> 00:07:10,875
[people gasping and murmuring]

157
00:07:11,708 --> 00:07:13,083
[officer] What a specimen.

158
00:07:13,916 --> 00:07:15,708
Hey, no need to be embarrassed.

159
00:07:15,791 --> 00:07:18,583
We've all been known to smoke
a little ganja after a long day.

160
00:07:20,333 --> 00:07:21,375
[Schmitti] Found it!

161
00:07:21,458 --> 00:07:22,458
[feet squeaking]

162
00:07:23,666 --> 00:07:24,875
Hoo.

163
00:07:26,916 --> 00:07:28,208
[clears throat]

164
00:07:31,416 --> 00:07:34,333
You got him an enormous weed plant?
What's wrong with you guys?

165
00:07:34,416 --> 00:07:36,416
What were you thinking?
He doesn't do that anymore.

166
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:38,583
You might as well be preteen delinquents,

167
00:07:38,666 --> 00:07:40,625
and I don't wanna see you
around Ralf again.

168
00:07:41,458 --> 00:07:42,291
Out!

169
00:07:49,708 --> 00:07:50,875
You better take off, guys.

170
00:07:50,958 --> 00:07:51,958
Hm.

171
00:07:55,458 --> 00:07:57,541
[Olli] Ugh… Schmitti's parties
used to be cooler.

172
00:07:57,625 --> 00:08:02,125
[Matze] Yeah, he's changed so much.
A year ago he was still a huge stoner.

173
00:08:02,208 --> 00:08:06,416
[Olli] Pff. And now he has a townhouse
with an ass shower. Thanks to Antje.

174
00:08:07,958 --> 00:08:10,750
Don't you think
some part of him actually wants this?

175
00:08:10,833 --> 00:08:13,833
No self-respecting man
would live like that.

176
00:08:13,916 --> 00:08:17,708
The guy just missed his opportunity
to escape that relationship. That's all.

177
00:08:19,958 --> 00:08:22,083
[Matze] Well, it's none of our business.

178
00:08:25,875 --> 00:08:27,500
We'll be shipping out soon anyway.

179
00:08:31,416 --> 00:08:32,333
[bottle opens]

180
00:08:32,916 --> 00:08:33,916
[Olli] Mm.

181
00:08:36,625 --> 00:08:37,875
Here's to our world tour.

182
00:08:39,625 --> 00:08:41,666
To the best time of our lives.

183
00:08:44,166 --> 00:08:45,166
[Olli] Mmm.

184
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:49,416
-[Matze] Great layout, right?
-[woman] Wow.

185
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:51,375
The previous tenant
even left some furniture here,

186
00:08:51,458 --> 00:08:52,958
in case you can make use of it.

187
00:08:53,541 --> 00:08:54,708
[woman] Wow.

188
00:08:58,416 --> 00:08:59,750
I could have it right away?

189
00:08:59,833 --> 00:09:00,750
Of course.

190
00:09:01,875 --> 00:09:03,958
[laughs] Perfect.

191
00:09:05,291 --> 00:09:07,375
I'm desperate to move,
I share an apartment.

192
00:09:07,458 --> 00:09:08,541
Having roommate problems?

193
00:09:08,625 --> 00:09:09,708
It's not that.

194
00:09:09,791 --> 00:09:11,750
My roommate's incredible.

195
00:09:11,833 --> 00:09:14,041
But, unfortunately, her boyfriend isn't.

196
00:09:14,125 --> 00:09:16,041
-He practically lives with us now.
-Got it.

197
00:09:16,125 --> 00:09:18,166
Pees on the top of the seat,
eats all your snacks.

198
00:09:18,250 --> 00:09:20,041
It's worse. He's clingy.

199
00:09:20,125 --> 00:09:23,500
She and I can barely hang out anymore
without him getting in the way.

200
00:09:23,583 --> 00:09:25,750
Plus, lately he likes to talk about
having kids with her

201
00:09:25,833 --> 00:09:27,041
while I'm sitting right there,

202
00:09:27,125 --> 00:09:30,416
so I thought, better get outta there
before they kick me to the curb.

203
00:09:31,125 --> 00:09:33,125
[romantic music playing]

204
00:09:34,875 --> 00:09:36,625
[both laughing]

205
00:09:38,083 --> 00:09:41,833
Um… be honest,
do you think I'll get the apartment?

206
00:09:42,791 --> 00:09:43,958
You can sign for it right now.

207
00:09:44,041 --> 00:09:45,041
Really?

208
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:48,708
Uh, wow.

209
00:10:00,791 --> 00:10:02,625
S-s-s-stop.

210
00:10:04,333 --> 00:10:06,416
Uh…

211
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:12,791
[stuttering] I should probably
tell you something, just before you sign.

212
00:10:14,125 --> 00:10:14,958
What is it?

213
00:10:15,041 --> 00:10:16,833
[stuttering] The…

214
00:10:16,916 --> 00:10:18,291
-Wait a second.
-The apartment…

215
00:10:18,375 --> 00:10:20,333
Uh… I had a stutter as a child.

216
00:10:20,416 --> 00:10:22,291
Trust me, there's a really simple trick.

217
00:10:22,375 --> 00:10:25,625
Just try whispering the words into…
into my ear like this, okay?

218
00:10:27,958 --> 00:10:30,583
[whispering] Th-this unit ha-has mold.

219
00:10:37,916 --> 00:10:39,500
I felt like I had to say something.

220
00:10:39,583 --> 00:10:40,833
Why show me this unit then?

221
00:10:40,916 --> 00:10:41,916
I…

222
00:10:42,833 --> 00:10:45,583
[stuttering] I wasn't…

223
00:10:54,208 --> 00:10:56,666
[softly] I didn't know
I'd like you this much, to be honest.

224
00:11:02,750 --> 00:11:05,291
Well, perfect timing.
I'm lucky you finally had that epiphany.

225
00:11:08,041 --> 00:11:08,875
You owe me for this.

226
00:11:09,916 --> 00:11:13,416
I'll, um… find you a terrific
new apartment as fast as I can.

227
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:15,958
I ha-have something in mind already.

228
00:11:16,541 --> 00:11:17,375
That too.

229
00:11:19,375 --> 00:11:21,416
I was also thinking
we could get some dinner.

230
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:22,833
Tonight?

231
00:11:23,875 --> 00:11:26,916
It won't be dinner
if we wait to eat 'til tomorrow morning.

232
00:11:35,708 --> 00:11:37,041
[door opens]

233
00:11:37,583 --> 00:11:38,958
-What's up?
-[door closes]

234
00:11:39,041 --> 00:11:42,333
The gym next to the grocery store
just got two cryotherapy chambers.

235
00:11:42,416 --> 00:11:44,583
Perfect for Iceland, we gotta be ready….

236
00:11:45,375 --> 00:11:47,416
Olli, this is Rebecca.

237
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:49,916
She viewed one of our units today.
[laughs]

238
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:51,166
Hi, it's a pleasure.

239
00:11:51,250 --> 00:11:52,458
Hey.

240
00:11:53,666 --> 00:11:54,875
Your bathroom, is it uh, over…?

241
00:11:54,958 --> 00:11:56,291
Oh, uh, over there on the right.

242
00:11:56,375 --> 00:11:57,333
Okay.

243
00:11:58,916 --> 00:11:59,916
A client?

244
00:12:00,625 --> 00:12:01,833
Oh, dude.

245
00:12:01,916 --> 00:12:04,083
She's amazing.

246
00:12:04,166 --> 00:12:06,500
Totally laid back and super sweet.

247
00:12:06,583 --> 00:12:09,166
And she's gonna be a dentist,
so she's got a great career too.

248
00:12:09,250 --> 00:12:11,500
We just had a lovely dinner.
Now we're going for a drink.

249
00:12:12,291 --> 00:12:13,125
Okay.

250
00:12:13,750 --> 00:12:16,458
Come get a beer with us.
You can get better acquainted.

251
00:12:16,541 --> 00:12:18,916
Dude, what's the point
in getting to know her?

252
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:20,750
We're gonna be gone in a few weeks.

253
00:12:23,750 --> 00:12:24,708
[Olli clears throat]

254
00:12:24,791 --> 00:12:27,166
Wow, your apartment is so charming.

255
00:12:27,708 --> 00:12:28,583
[Olli] Mm.

256
00:12:29,125 --> 00:12:31,250
So huge. [laughs]

257
00:12:32,500 --> 00:12:34,291
That's the route
for our around-the-world trip.

258
00:12:34,375 --> 00:12:36,666
Did Matze mention
we'll be leaving for a year pretty soon?

259
00:12:36,750 --> 00:12:39,666
Yeah, yeah, he did.
I did a trip like that before college.

260
00:12:39,750 --> 00:12:41,000
-[Matze] Oh really?
-Yeah, I did…

261
00:12:41,083 --> 00:12:45,625
two months in Australia and New Zealand,
then I spent another month in Thailand.

262
00:12:46,375 --> 00:12:47,458
[laughing]

263
00:12:47,541 --> 00:12:50,291
Three months? Are you serious?
That's hardly a world tour.

264
00:12:50,875 --> 00:12:53,208
You gotta set foot on every continent
at the very least.

265
00:12:54,416 --> 00:12:55,708
Okay. [laughs]

266
00:12:56,375 --> 00:12:57,916
Okay, how 'bout that drink?

267
00:12:58,000 --> 00:12:59,666
-Yeah.
-[Olli] Where we going?

268
00:13:02,583 --> 00:13:03,666
What's wrong, dude?

269
00:13:04,458 --> 00:13:06,666
A minute ago,
you asked me if I wanted to come.

270
00:13:08,083 --> 00:13:09,291
True. I did.

271
00:13:09,375 --> 00:13:12,625
Well then, let's go!
I'm going stir-crazy in here by myself.

272
00:13:12,708 --> 00:13:14,583
["Happy" by Pharrell Williams playing]

273
00:13:14,666 --> 00:13:17,666
<i>♪ It might seem crazy</i>
<i>What I'm 'bout to say ♪</i>

274
00:13:18,250 --> 00:13:20,541
-Hi, Olli.
-Hey.

275
00:13:21,500 --> 00:13:22,708
Hey.

276
00:13:23,666 --> 00:13:25,875
[Rebecca] Good! No, really. I'm impressed.

277
00:13:25,958 --> 00:13:27,666
[Matze] Check this out.
Gonna blow your mind.

278
00:13:27,750 --> 00:13:30,041
<i>♪ …balloon that could go to space ♪</i>

279
00:13:31,833 --> 00:13:35,708
<i>♪With the air, like I don't care</i>
<i>Baby, by the way ♪</i>

280
00:13:35,791 --> 00:13:37,166
That's my favorite. Blueberries.

281
00:13:37,250 --> 00:13:39,041
-They seemed gross as a kid.
-[song continues]

282
00:13:39,125 --> 00:13:40,666
Oh, look. Morning, sunshine.

283
00:13:40,750 --> 00:13:42,291
Hey, do you want a little?

284
00:13:43,375 --> 00:13:44,583
<i>♪ 'Because I'm happy ♪</i>

285
00:13:44,666 --> 00:13:49,291
<i>♪ Clap along if you feel</i>
<i>That happiness is the truth ♪</i>

286
00:13:49,375 --> 00:13:50,958
<i>♪ 'Because I'm happy ♪</i>

287
00:13:51,041 --> 00:13:54,916
<i>♪ Clap along if you know</i>
<i>What happiness is to you ♪</i>

288
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,125
[screaming]

289
00:13:57,208 --> 00:14:00,458
Hey! Hey, I'm in the shower,
turn off the sink!

290
00:14:00,541 --> 00:14:01,500
Sorry!

291
00:14:02,833 --> 00:14:05,541
<i>♪ Here come bad news</i>
<i>Talking this and that ♪</i>

292
00:14:05,625 --> 00:14:07,208
-[Rebecca] Yeah.
-[Matze] I know.

293
00:14:07,291 --> 00:14:09,666
Right? It couldn't hurt.
You know, to go jogging again sometime.

294
00:14:09,750 --> 00:14:11,958
Fine. I'll tag along. Hey.

295
00:14:12,958 --> 00:14:14,125
Hey!

296
00:14:14,666 --> 00:14:16,250
<i>♪ I should probably warn you ♪</i>

297
00:14:16,333 --> 00:14:17,875
<i>-♪ I'll be just fine ♪</i>
-Gotcha.

298
00:14:19,041 --> 00:14:19,875
Come on!

299
00:14:20,666 --> 00:14:23,750
<i>♪ No offense to you</i>
<i>Don't waste your time ♪</i>

300
00:14:24,250 --> 00:14:25,291
<i>♪ Here's why ♪</i>

301
00:14:25,375 --> 00:14:26,666
<i>♪ Because I'm happy ♪</i>

302
00:14:26,750 --> 00:14:30,833
<i>♪ Clap along if you feel</i>
<i>Like a room without a roof ♪</i>

303
00:14:30,916 --> 00:14:31,875
[Matze] Are you sure?

304
00:14:31,958 --> 00:14:35,083
[Rebecca] Trust me.
You're gonna look amazing.

305
00:14:35,166 --> 00:14:37,250
<i>♪ …like happiness is the truth ♪</i>

306
00:14:37,333 --> 00:14:38,666
<i>♪ Because I'm happy ♪</i>

307
00:14:39,166 --> 00:14:40,916
-See? That's perfect.
-That one.

308
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:42,083
Oh, wait.

309
00:14:42,750 --> 00:14:43,583
[exhales]

310
00:14:43,666 --> 00:14:44,916
Okay.

311
00:14:46,333 --> 00:14:48,625
Perfect. That looks great.

312
00:14:49,291 --> 00:14:50,583
MEXICO AND THE DÍA DE LOS MUERTOS

313
00:14:50,666 --> 00:14:52,375
[Matze] Did you ask
if they have a spot for us?

314
00:14:52,458 --> 00:14:53,541
Matze.

315
00:14:53,625 --> 00:14:56,458
<i>-♪ …bring me down ♪</i>
-Rebecca.

316
00:14:56,541 --> 00:14:59,666
-Yeah. Oh yeah, for sure.
<i>-♪ Bring me down, can't nothin' ♪</i>

317
00:14:59,750 --> 00:15:03,166
<i>♪ Bring me down</i>
<i>I said I tell you now, uh! ♪</i>

318
00:15:03,250 --> 00:15:05,250
<i>♪ Bring me down, can't nothin' ♪</i>

319
00:15:06,041 --> 00:15:07,625
[humming]

320
00:15:07,708 --> 00:15:09,333
MEXICO
DISCOVER THE COUNTRY AND ITS PEOPLE

321
00:15:11,958 --> 00:15:13,958
[intriguing music playing]

322
00:15:24,583 --> 00:15:28,416
[continues humming]

323
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:41,125
[imitating Antje] Ralf!

324
00:15:43,791 --> 00:15:47,833
Uh… Is, uh… is Antje here or what?

325
00:15:47,916 --> 00:15:50,791
Ya know, that was
a stupid thing to do the other day.

326
00:15:50,875 --> 00:15:53,291
-I can't betray Antje for you two.
-Ah, spare me.

327
00:15:53,375 --> 00:15:55,833
Wait, man, why'd you just
put a muffin in your jacket?

328
00:15:55,916 --> 00:15:59,583
Oh, fuck. Uh, Antje's been dieting,
I'm supposed to support her. Hold this.

329
00:15:59,666 --> 00:16:01,375
[plastic rustling]

330
00:16:01,458 --> 00:16:03,500
Shit, she can't know
I was eating chocolate.

331
00:16:04,791 --> 00:16:05,750
[rubbing fabric]

332
00:16:07,791 --> 00:16:09,875
Yeah, relationships are
about compromise, you know?

333
00:16:09,958 --> 00:16:13,333
[scoffs] A compromise is
screwing the cap on the toothpaste.

334
00:16:13,416 --> 00:16:16,916
I mean, you're giving up your whole
personality, for chrissakes. Ugh.

335
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:20,458
With you, I'm not shocked. But I didn't
think Matze would fall into this trap.

336
00:16:20,541 --> 00:16:22,666
-How much will it take for him to realize…
-What happened?

337
00:16:22,750 --> 00:16:25,916
It's his new girlfriend,
the poor man is just brainwashed.

338
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,791
Like, he actually started dieting,
now he's going jogging!

339
00:16:28,875 --> 00:16:30,500
No time for me anymore. Geez!

340
00:16:30,583 --> 00:16:32,500
Well yeah, it's a lost cause
competing with a girl.

341
00:16:32,583 --> 00:16:33,541
[scoffs]

342
00:16:36,958 --> 00:16:38,958
She's been planning a party
for a few days.

343
00:16:39,041 --> 00:16:42,083
She aced some stupid dentist test.
Apparently we're hosting!

344
00:16:46,500 --> 00:16:47,458
Come out for it.

345
00:16:47,541 --> 00:16:49,333
Antje won't let me go to yours.

346
00:16:49,416 --> 00:16:53,541
Ugh! She doesn't have to know
you're coming to the party. Hm?

347
00:16:56,125 --> 00:16:57,125
We'll see.

348
00:16:57,208 --> 00:16:59,458
-[lively music playing]
-[party chatter]

349
00:16:59,541 --> 00:17:02,125
Yeah, so, we actually went
to the same school.

350
00:17:02,208 --> 00:17:04,875
-Um, and we go back a ways. Yeah.
-Okay, interesting.

351
00:17:04,958 --> 00:17:08,125
Excuse me, a teeny tiny second.
That's Moni, my roommate.

352
00:17:08,208 --> 00:17:09,083
-Ah.
-Sorry.

353
00:17:09,166 --> 00:17:10,083
Excuse me.

354
00:17:10,166 --> 00:17:12,083
-[clears throat]
-[Moni] Hey!

355
00:17:12,166 --> 00:17:13,083
[Rebecca] Moni.

356
00:17:13,166 --> 00:17:14,041
-Hello.
-Matze.

357
00:17:14,125 --> 00:17:15,583
-Hey, nice to meet you.
-Likewise.

358
00:17:16,833 --> 00:17:18,166
So where's your Siamese twin, huh?

359
00:17:18,750 --> 00:17:20,208
Hauke can't be here tonight,

360
00:17:20,291 --> 00:17:22,416
-he caught a bad case of the flu.
-Hmm.

361
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:24,666
-I told him to call if he needs anything.
-[phone buzzes]

362
00:17:24,750 --> 00:17:26,416
Oops, guess who it is.

363
00:17:26,500 --> 00:17:28,625
Hey, my sweet little snuggle bunny.

364
00:17:28,708 --> 00:17:31,041
-What's going on?
-[Hauke coughs] <i>I have really bad chills.</i>

365
00:17:31,125 --> 00:17:35,083
<i>And the fever's back and it's worse</i>
<i>than before, it's already at 99.2.</i>

366
00:17:35,166 --> 00:17:38,750
<i>-I'm dying here. I can't do this.</i>
-Hauke, hey, so glad you called actually,

367
00:17:38,833 --> 00:17:40,333
because I just read a major study

368
00:17:40,416 --> 00:17:44,000
that said the use of mobile devices can
exacerbate the flu in certain cases, okay?

369
00:17:44,083 --> 00:17:47,625
Grab some aspirin and stay in bed.
Hey, get well soon. <i>Ciao!</i>

370
00:17:48,833 --> 00:17:49,875
I'll grab you a drink.

371
00:17:49,958 --> 00:17:50,833
Yeah, thanks.

372
00:17:53,708 --> 00:17:56,791
-Where did you tell Antje you were going?
-I didn't.

373
00:17:56,875 --> 00:18:00,125
I waited for her to leave for the movies
with a bunch of friends of hers.

374
00:18:00,208 --> 00:18:01,833
I can't be out past ten o'clock.

375
00:18:03,750 --> 00:18:05,083
Really? That's only an hour.

376
00:18:05,166 --> 00:18:06,458
Well, that's my life.

377
00:18:06,541 --> 00:18:08,166
It takes me a full 50 minutes to get home.

378
00:18:08,250 --> 00:18:09,833
-Suburbia is overrated.
-Mm.

379
00:18:12,333 --> 00:18:14,583
Oh, uh… your plant's upstairs, ya know.

380
00:18:15,125 --> 00:18:17,208
Yeah, so? I quit that stuff.

381
00:18:17,291 --> 00:18:19,625
So, um… you can watch me smoke. Hm?

382
00:18:19,708 --> 00:18:20,708
Come on.

383
00:18:21,791 --> 00:18:23,666
Okay, but I won't touch it.

384
00:18:23,750 --> 00:18:24,625
[lighter flicks]

385
00:18:24,708 --> 00:18:26,708
[inhaling]

386
00:18:36,083 --> 00:18:38,333
[coughing]

387
00:18:38,416 --> 00:18:40,416
[Olli laughs]

388
00:18:42,666 --> 00:18:44,958
-Rebecca? Um…
-[doorbell rings]

389
00:18:45,041 --> 00:18:47,041
Alonso's here, at the door.

390
00:18:48,541 --> 00:18:49,583
My boss is here.

391
00:18:49,666 --> 00:18:51,000
Your boss got an invite?

392
00:18:52,833 --> 00:18:55,791
Yeah, I told him just to be polite.
I didn't think he'd actually show up.

393
00:18:58,250 --> 00:18:59,083
Hello!

394
00:18:59,166 --> 00:19:00,416
Miss Richthoff.

395
00:19:00,500 --> 00:19:02,541
I brought a little gift
for you this evening.

396
00:19:03,500 --> 00:19:04,333
Do you like wine?

397
00:19:04,416 --> 00:19:06,958
Forgive the ridiculous question.
Of course you like it.

398
00:19:07,041 --> 00:19:09,250
I can't think of anybody who doesn't.
It's wonderful.

399
00:19:09,333 --> 00:19:12,208
And that means Mr. Dommel
from the Pfaffenbeck winery

400
00:19:12,291 --> 00:19:14,250
is certainly in good company.

401
00:19:14,333 --> 00:19:15,375
Okay, Mr. Dommel.

402
00:19:15,875 --> 00:19:17,166
Good evening, hello.

403
00:19:17,250 --> 00:19:18,291
-Hello.
-Right.

404
00:19:18,791 --> 00:19:21,333
Don't you think a wine tasting
would add something special tonight,

405
00:19:21,416 --> 00:19:22,750
a whiff of refinement, perhaps?

406
00:19:22,833 --> 00:19:25,458
Of course you do.
Everybody loves wine at a party.

407
00:19:25,541 --> 00:19:26,750
Mr., uh, Dommel, go on.

408
00:19:27,750 --> 00:19:28,625
[uncertain laugh]

409
00:19:32,791 --> 00:19:34,208
Hey, is peeing still okay up here?

410
00:19:35,666 --> 00:19:37,000
Yeah.

411
00:19:37,083 --> 00:19:38,208
Who just randomly sends

412
00:19:38,291 --> 00:19:41,833
a wine-tasting pop-up to a party
with a whole winemaker thrown in?

413
00:19:41,916 --> 00:19:43,958
Uh, well,
would a magician have been better?

414
00:19:44,041 --> 00:19:46,500
We could hire one
to make the wine guy disappear.

415
00:19:47,166 --> 00:19:51,083
I'll show Mr. Dommel where he can set up,
and we'll, uh… be fine, okay?

416
00:19:51,958 --> 00:19:55,208
Okay, I'll get my boss squared away.
Thank you.

417
00:19:57,625 --> 00:20:01,416
Miss Richthoff, do you have a private area
where I could make a quick phone call?

418
00:20:02,291 --> 00:20:04,375
Um, yeah, absolutely, I'll show you.

419
00:20:05,083 --> 00:20:08,000
-[sighs]
-[urine splashing]

420
00:20:10,666 --> 00:20:13,125
[Rebecca] Okay, here we are.
You can definitely…

421
00:20:13,208 --> 00:20:15,500
-Thank you.
-…take your phone call out here.

422
00:20:16,166 --> 00:20:17,750
[tense music playing]

423
00:20:18,333 --> 00:20:19,583
Oh, sorry.

424
00:20:19,666 --> 00:20:21,041
[laughing]

425
00:20:24,916 --> 00:20:25,916
Uh…

426
00:20:26,750 --> 00:20:28,500
I'd recommend a prostate exam.

427
00:20:28,583 --> 00:20:31,125
Your stream looks
awfully feeble and inconsistent.

428
00:20:31,208 --> 00:20:35,625
Uh, um… maybe we should find
a different place, somewhere downstairs.

429
00:20:39,625 --> 00:20:44,916
[both laughing]

430
00:20:46,083 --> 00:20:48,500
-He walked right into the splash zone!
-I got his shoes!

431
00:20:48,583 --> 00:20:50,250
Hope they're moisture-wicking.

432
00:20:50,333 --> 00:20:51,708
[laughing]

433
00:20:52,416 --> 00:20:54,166
Thanks a lot. I work for him.

434
00:20:54,250 --> 00:20:56,750
Wow, hey, cool boss.
How come you didn't introduce us?

435
00:20:56,833 --> 00:21:01,041
Hey. Uh, do me a favor, okay?
Please stay up here for tonight. Thanks.

436
00:21:01,125 --> 00:21:02,875
-She must be nuts.
-[door closes]

437
00:21:02,958 --> 00:21:04,250
This is my house, dude!

438
00:21:05,125 --> 00:21:06,875
-Your place is killer.
-Matze?

439
00:21:07,541 --> 00:21:09,833
That guy Schmitti
just took a leak on my boss's shoes.

440
00:21:09,916 --> 00:21:11,791
-What?
-He and Olli are smoking weed out there.

441
00:21:11,875 --> 00:21:14,000
I thought Schmitti didn't smoke anymore.

442
00:21:14,083 --> 00:21:15,916
Huh? That's not the issue here.

443
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:17,500
What did Alonso say after that?

444
00:21:18,250 --> 00:21:21,458
[sighs] That I have an interesting
circle of friends in my life.

445
00:21:23,208 --> 00:21:25,125
Seems like you missed
an opportunity to tell him

446
00:21:25,208 --> 00:21:27,750
that homeless guys sneak up there
every now and then.

447
00:21:27,833 --> 00:21:30,916
Good idea, but it's a bit too late, Matze.

448
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,833
I told them that they should
probably stay upstairs tonight.

449
00:21:34,583 --> 00:21:36,041
Oh, boy.

450
00:21:36,125 --> 00:21:38,208
You may have just lit a fuse in Olli.

451
00:21:38,291 --> 00:21:40,250
["Here Comes the Hotstepper"
by Ini Kamoze playing]

452
00:21:40,333 --> 00:21:46,416
<i>♪ Na, na-na-na-na, na-na-na-na</i>
<i>Na-na-na, na-na-na, na-na-na-na ♪</i>

453
00:21:48,291 --> 00:21:50,583
<i>-♪ Here comes the hotstepper ♪</i>
<i>-♪ Murderer ♪</i>

454
00:21:50,666 --> 00:21:53,250
<i>-♪ I'm the lyrical gangster ♪</i>
<i>-♪ Murderer ♪</i>

455
00:21:53,333 --> 00:21:55,583
<i>-♪ Big up di crew ina di area ♪</i>
<i>-♪ Murderer ♪</i>

456
00:21:55,666 --> 00:21:58,500
<i>-♪ Still love you like that ♪</i>
<i>-♪ Murderer ♪</i>

457
00:21:59,625 --> 00:22:01,708
I'll make sure
they don't do something dumb.

458
00:22:01,791 --> 00:22:04,625
<i>♪ Yes, we multiply ♪</i>

459
00:22:04,708 --> 00:22:08,500
<i>♪ Anyone press will hear</i>
<i>The fat lady sing ♪</i>

460
00:22:09,083 --> 00:22:11,250
<i>♪ Act like you know, RICO ♪</i>

461
00:22:11,333 --> 00:22:13,750
<i>♪ I know what Bo don't know ♪</i>

462
00:22:13,833 --> 00:22:17,291
<i>♪ Touch them up and go, uh-oh!</i>
<i>Ch-ch-chang, chang ♪</i>

463
00:22:17,375 --> 00:22:19,541
<i>-♪ Here comes the hotstepper ♪</i>
<i>-♪ Murderer ♪</i>

464
00:22:19,625 --> 00:22:22,083
<i>-♪ I'm the lyrical gangster ♪</i>
<i>-♪ Murderer ♪</i>

465
00:22:22,166 --> 00:22:24,625
<i>-♪ Excuse me, Mr. Officer ♪</i>
<i>-♪ Murderer ♪</i>

466
00:22:24,708 --> 00:22:27,791
<i>-♪ Still love you like that ♪</i>
<i>-♪ Murderer ♪</i>

467
00:22:29,166 --> 00:22:30,958
All right. I believe I'm all set.

468
00:22:31,041 --> 00:22:32,333
-Just a sec…
-He'll hook you up.

469
00:22:34,750 --> 00:22:35,833
[clinking]

470
00:22:37,083 --> 00:22:43,125
Hey, you guys! Um, this is, um, Mr. Dommel
from a winery called, um, Pfaffenbeck.

471
00:22:43,208 --> 00:22:47,916
And tonight he's here to introduce you
to the, uh, wonderful world of winemaking.

472
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:49,416
-Right? Uh, perfect.
-Yeah.

473
00:22:49,500 --> 00:22:51,125
Thanks a lot and let's have some fun!

474
00:22:55,250 --> 00:22:58,583
All right, well, how 'bout we dive in? Uh…

475
00:22:59,208 --> 00:23:02,208
The important thing here,
as with any natural product,

476
00:23:02,291 --> 00:23:04,875
is the quality
of the source materials and ingredients.

477
00:23:04,958 --> 00:23:08,375
At Pfaffenbeck we use organic grapes,
uh, grown responsibly.

478
00:23:08,458 --> 00:23:11,125
And for our toast we use
free-range white bread, folks!

479
00:23:11,208 --> 00:23:13,750
[uncomfortable laughter and murmuring]

480
00:23:14,416 --> 00:23:18,500
Right, so we don't use any pesticides
or any fungicides or any herbicides.

481
00:23:18,583 --> 00:23:23,083
Oh, and our sandwiches are guaranteed
vitamin and nutrient free.

482
00:23:23,166 --> 00:23:24,083
Olli, don't.

483
00:23:24,166 --> 00:23:26,875
[Dommel] There are many elaborate
and protracted steps in between,

484
00:23:26,958 --> 00:23:30,500
from harvesting the fresh berries
to… bottling the product and selling it.

485
00:23:30,583 --> 00:23:32,458
[Olli] If you get carried away
with a bottle

486
00:23:32,541 --> 00:23:34,083
and you need to sober up, we recommend…

487
00:23:35,166 --> 00:23:36,583
-sandwiches.
-Sandwiches.

488
00:23:36,666 --> 00:23:38,125
[laughter]

489
00:23:38,916 --> 00:23:40,708
Why don't I open a bottle right now, huh?

490
00:23:40,791 --> 00:23:42,875
Pew-pew-pew-pew-pew-pew-pew!

491
00:23:42,958 --> 00:23:45,500
Rebecca, do you think
you could take care of the disruption?

492
00:23:45,583 --> 00:23:48,416
I'd be very grateful.
Everyone would be very grateful.

493
00:23:48,500 --> 00:23:49,583
Yes, I'm on it, of course.

494
00:23:50,208 --> 00:23:51,083
Thank you.

495
00:23:52,250 --> 00:23:53,541
-Sound good?
-Umm. Mm-hmm.

496
00:23:59,875 --> 00:24:01,416
Olli. That's enough.

497
00:24:02,041 --> 00:24:03,875
-Hey stop. That's mine!
-Stop.

498
00:24:03,958 --> 00:24:05,833
-Goddammit, that's enough, okay?
-Hey. Give me--

499
00:24:05,916 --> 00:24:06,958
I said stop it!

500
00:24:07,041 --> 00:24:08,125
-[Olli grunts]
-[Rebecca] Oh!

501
00:24:08,208 --> 00:24:09,041
[people gasp]

502
00:24:10,833 --> 00:24:12,791
Don't panic! No big deal.

503
00:24:13,583 --> 00:24:15,333
She's still intact!

504
00:24:15,416 --> 00:24:16,583
Sandwiches all around!

505
00:24:16,666 --> 00:24:19,166
[Schmitti] Yeah! Whoo!

506
00:24:19,250 --> 00:24:20,583
C'mon!

507
00:24:20,666 --> 00:24:21,666
It's okay.

508
00:24:24,333 --> 00:24:25,583
When were you going to step in?

509
00:24:25,666 --> 00:24:29,041
When he's swinging from the ceiling
or when he sets the place on fire?

510
00:24:29,125 --> 00:24:30,291
I'm sorry, okay?

511
00:24:30,375 --> 00:24:32,375
Olli can be a menace
when he's been drinking.

512
00:24:32,458 --> 00:24:34,125
Trust me, he doesn't mean you harm.

513
00:24:34,208 --> 00:24:37,541
Oh, cool. It changes everything
if he's not doing it on purpose.

514
00:24:38,291 --> 00:24:40,208
We'll get this back under control.

515
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:45,916
Okay! Welcome to the best game ever.

516
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,125
Each player has to have
extremely strong nerves.

517
00:24:48,208 --> 00:24:49,875
-Hold up, hang on, don't turn it over.
-Oh.

518
00:24:49,958 --> 00:24:51,750
Hurry up. We're all waiting here.

519
00:24:51,833 --> 00:24:55,458
Okay, so this is a game my dear friend
Matze and I created from scratch,

520
00:24:55,541 --> 00:24:57,750
after many weeks of contemplation.

521
00:24:57,833 --> 00:25:00,375
-The legendary…
-[drumming]

522
00:25:00,458 --> 00:25:02,041
Dookie Quartet.

523
00:25:03,208 --> 00:25:05,833
-[Schmitti] Get ready.
-Okay, look at your cards. Mm?

524
00:25:06,458 --> 00:25:08,000
[people exclaiming]

525
00:25:08,083 --> 00:25:10,833
Holy moly! That's a real whopper.

526
00:25:12,250 --> 00:25:13,625
And so symmetrical.

527
00:25:13,708 --> 00:25:15,291
Thanks. It's mine.

528
00:25:15,375 --> 00:25:18,625
I haven't seen a pile of crap like that
since my college dorm days.

529
00:25:18,708 --> 00:25:20,750
Mine tend to be more like potato soup now.

530
00:25:20,833 --> 00:25:22,250
Don't be so hard on yourself.

531
00:25:22,333 --> 00:25:25,208
I made that after Matze and I
went on that all-bean diet.

532
00:25:25,833 --> 00:25:27,458
-No kidding.
-[people gasp]

533
00:25:28,750 --> 00:25:30,083
Moni! Hey.

534
00:25:30,916 --> 00:25:31,916
Hey.

535
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:33,250
-Can you get up?
-Uh, yeah.

536
00:25:33,333 --> 00:25:34,458
-[Schmitti] Nice.
-Yeah.

537
00:25:34,541 --> 00:25:35,666
-Up you go.
-Time for a shot.

538
00:25:35,750 --> 00:25:37,000
[Dommel] I still have to drive.

539
00:25:37,083 --> 00:25:40,833
Let's go sit down. Here. Hey. Look at me.

540
00:25:40,916 --> 00:25:41,875
Are you okay?

541
00:25:42,416 --> 00:25:43,291
Uh, yeah…

542
00:25:44,208 --> 00:25:45,500
You can go, it's all right.

543
00:25:46,250 --> 00:25:49,666
Hm. So this basically works
like normal Quartet, or…?

544
00:25:49,750 --> 00:25:51,000
You outta your mind, dude?

545
00:25:51,083 --> 00:25:52,083
Calm down.

546
00:25:52,166 --> 00:25:54,666
Next round we'll let you play.
As I was saying,

547
00:25:54,750 --> 00:25:58,416
like in a normal game of Quartet,
the highest value wins.

548
00:25:58,500 --> 00:26:01,041
As for toilet paper,
do your best to get rid of it.

549
00:26:01,125 --> 00:26:02,291
And the "stink factor"?

550
00:26:02,375 --> 00:26:03,583
Maximizes your points.

551
00:26:03,666 --> 00:26:05,458
But stinking is usually negative, right?

552
00:26:05,541 --> 00:26:07,750
Eh, not when you're dealing with turds.

553
00:26:07,833 --> 00:26:11,541
All right, I'm, uh,
going for artistic value, 8.5.

554
00:26:14,625 --> 00:26:16,000
-The pretzel.
-The pretzel.

555
00:26:17,458 --> 00:26:19,000
You're not sick. You need water.

556
00:26:19,083 --> 00:26:20,166
-Stay here.
-Sorry, Becky,

557
00:26:20,250 --> 00:26:21,541
uh, do you need anything?

558
00:26:21,625 --> 00:26:24,041
-Yeah, water for Moni. No, it's okay.
-I can get it. Right?

559
00:26:24,125 --> 00:26:25,916
[man] Wow… never seen one like that.

560
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:27,500
What's so entertaining over here?

561
00:26:27,583 --> 00:26:29,291
We're playing Dookie Quartet.

562
00:26:29,875 --> 00:26:31,375
This is Matze's masterpiece.

563
00:26:32,500 --> 00:26:34,666
We don't stand a chance
against the pretzel.

564
00:26:36,875 --> 00:26:38,541
On that note, I have to go.

565
00:26:38,625 --> 00:26:41,041
Thank you all
for such an interesting evening.

566
00:26:41,583 --> 00:26:42,875
Uh, I'm sorry, Dr. Alonso…

567
00:26:42,958 --> 00:26:43,875
Please, don't be sorry.

568
00:26:43,958 --> 00:26:47,541
I love looking at high-quality photos
of fecal artwork.

569
00:26:48,208 --> 00:26:49,500
Two or three are pretty alarming.

570
00:26:49,583 --> 00:26:52,416
Consider seeing a specialist
or eating more fiber. Have a nice evening.

571
00:26:53,458 --> 00:26:54,791
Shit. Wait, sir!

572
00:26:56,916 --> 00:26:58,833
Oh! Here's to driving!

573
00:26:58,916 --> 00:27:00,291
[Rebecca] Dr. Alonso!

574
00:27:00,375 --> 00:27:01,416
See you Monday.

575
00:27:03,583 --> 00:27:04,458
Sh…

576
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:06,000
I'm so sorry about this.

577
00:27:06,083 --> 00:27:08,000
Tonight was supposed to be
a nice, calm evening

578
00:27:08,083 --> 00:27:10,125
with my new colleagues, you understand?

579
00:27:10,208 --> 00:27:12,208
Instead, my boss saw
your "greatest shits".

580
00:27:12,291 --> 00:27:14,333
Which means that I saw your shit.

581
00:27:14,416 --> 00:27:16,333
And everyone here saw your shit too!

582
00:27:16,416 --> 00:27:17,916
It was a shitty party.

583
00:27:19,041 --> 00:27:21,041
["Sugar on Your Tongue" by Silver playing]

584
00:27:27,083 --> 00:27:28,166
[door slams]

585
00:27:31,500 --> 00:27:35,875
<i>♪ Make me say ah ♪</i>

586
00:27:35,958 --> 00:27:38,666
<i>♪ La, la-la-la ♪</i>

587
00:27:38,750 --> 00:27:41,291
<i>♪ Make me say ah ♪</i>

588
00:27:42,875 --> 00:27:45,875
<i>♪ La, la-la-la ♪</i>

589
00:27:46,625 --> 00:27:49,500
<i>♪ Ah ♪</i>

590
00:27:49,583 --> 00:27:51,916
<i>♪ La, la-la-la ♪</i>

591
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:56,250
<i>♪ Make me say ah ♪</i>

592
00:27:56,750 --> 00:27:59,833
<i>♪ La, la-la-la ♪</i>

593
00:28:01,458 --> 00:28:06,708
[phone vibrating]

594
00:28:09,166 --> 00:28:12,208
[phone continues vibrating]

595
00:28:16,875 --> 00:28:19,208
Holy shit! Oh, fuck!

596
00:28:21,625 --> 00:28:22,458
283 MISSED CALLS

597
00:28:22,541 --> 00:28:25,458
Fuck, fuck, fuck, fuck, fuck, fuck.
Antje tried to call me 283 times.

598
00:28:25,541 --> 00:28:26,583
[Olli groans]

599
00:28:26,666 --> 00:28:28,875
The first time, you let the phone ring.

600
00:28:28,958 --> 00:28:31,125
Then you just ignored it
and put it on mute.

601
00:28:31,208 --> 00:28:33,208
What? You should've told me to answer!

602
00:28:33,291 --> 00:28:35,458
I did, but you yelled,
"I don't wanna leave yet."

603
00:28:35,541 --> 00:28:37,083
"I wish that nag would leave me alone."

604
00:28:37,166 --> 00:28:39,333
What? Oh, shit.

605
00:28:39,416 --> 00:28:41,291
Oh, dammit.

606
00:28:42,708 --> 00:28:43,750
Idiot. Screw you.

607
00:28:44,291 --> 00:28:45,375
[door closes]

608
00:28:45,458 --> 00:28:46,416
That poor guy.

609
00:28:46,500 --> 00:28:48,125
[running footsteps receding]

610
00:28:48,208 --> 00:28:49,708
-[man groaning]
-[distant door slams]

611
00:28:50,333 --> 00:28:51,333
[groaning continues]

612
00:28:53,375 --> 00:28:57,125
Oh my God. Was it as good for you
as it was for me? Hm?

613
00:28:58,541 --> 00:29:00,583
-You're such an idiot.
-[Olli laughs]

614
00:29:01,250 --> 00:29:03,583
Ugh… Ow, my head's exploding.

615
00:29:04,416 --> 00:29:07,541
That's what happens when you eat healthy.
You get a weak stomach.

616
00:29:09,500 --> 00:29:10,791
How come you're in my bed?

617
00:29:10,875 --> 00:29:13,041
Becky just wanted some peace.

618
00:29:13,125 --> 00:29:14,166
[Olli scoffs]

619
00:29:16,541 --> 00:29:18,125
[grunts and exhales]

620
00:29:18,208 --> 00:29:20,791
[both grunting and groaning]

621
00:29:22,666 --> 00:29:25,750
-[Olli] Ugh.
-Look, man, what was that last night?

622
00:29:28,500 --> 00:29:31,083
It was Becky's night,
and you completely ruined it.

623
00:29:31,166 --> 00:29:33,541
Uh, what? How so?

624
00:29:34,541 --> 00:29:36,291
It was an amazing party.

625
00:29:36,375 --> 00:29:39,000
Dookie Quartet was a big hit, just saying.

626
00:29:39,083 --> 00:29:40,041
[Matze] Oh shit.

627
00:29:40,125 --> 00:29:42,125
That explains
Becky's freak-out last night.

628
00:29:42,208 --> 00:29:45,333
Hey, listen, you should get back
to finding her an apartment.

629
00:29:45,416 --> 00:29:47,000
She's here way too much, man.

630
00:29:47,083 --> 00:29:48,666
[exhales]

631
00:29:48,750 --> 00:29:52,625
Well, I'm gonna go sit upstairs in the sun
and have a little hair-of-the-dog.

632
00:29:52,708 --> 00:29:53,666
You can join me later.

633
00:29:53,750 --> 00:29:58,875
[metallic scraping]

634
00:29:59,583 --> 00:30:00,458
Ugh.

635
00:30:06,500 --> 00:30:07,625
Hi.

636
00:30:08,625 --> 00:30:11,416
Just wanted to… say I'm sorry again.

637
00:30:12,458 --> 00:30:13,916
Don't talk to me right now.

638
00:30:17,125 --> 00:30:18,541
Okay, I'll just be here.

639
00:30:24,291 --> 00:30:25,833
Look, Matze, we need to talk.

640
00:30:25,916 --> 00:30:28,000
Do you have any idea how annoying this is?

641
00:30:28,083 --> 00:30:31,583
I live in your apartment because
Hauke and Moni are driving me crazy,

642
00:30:31,666 --> 00:30:34,750
and now I'm stuck
in that exact situation, but with Olli.

643
00:30:34,833 --> 00:30:37,041
I can't do these three-way relationships,
you know?

644
00:30:37,125 --> 00:30:39,166
Olli is way cooler than Hauke.

645
00:30:39,791 --> 00:30:41,291
Okay, that's not the point I'm making.

646
00:30:42,416 --> 00:30:43,375
[sighs]

647
00:30:48,208 --> 00:30:49,333
C'mon, we need to solve this.

648
00:30:49,416 --> 00:30:52,333
I can't… just, like,
force Olli to move out, though.

649
00:30:52,416 --> 00:30:54,125
Uh, right, that's not…

650
00:30:54,750 --> 00:30:56,375
Of course not, but…

651
00:30:56,458 --> 00:30:58,333
you could leave anytime.

652
00:30:59,458 --> 00:31:01,041
Uh… what I mean is,

653
00:31:01,625 --> 00:31:05,208
Right now we're looking for a place
that's… that's, you know, just for me.

654
00:31:06,416 --> 00:31:07,625
Matze.

655
00:31:07,708 --> 00:31:11,833
With most other men,
any mention of… of moving in together…

656
00:31:11,916 --> 00:31:12,916
[emotional music playing]

657
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:15,833
…would've made me have a panic attack,
but not with you.

658
00:31:17,875 --> 00:31:20,000
Uh, sorry, was there
a compliment in there at all?

659
00:31:20,083 --> 00:31:21,333
[laughs]

660
00:31:25,041 --> 00:31:25,875
Okay, so?

661
00:31:26,541 --> 00:31:27,666
Do you wanna shack up?

662
00:31:28,583 --> 00:31:31,291
Uh, if you, um, you know…
think you'd be interested.

663
00:31:31,375 --> 00:31:32,666
Yeah.

664
00:31:33,166 --> 00:31:35,458
We're getting an apartment. [laughs]

665
00:31:36,416 --> 00:31:37,541
-C'mere.
-Oh.

666
00:31:47,500 --> 00:31:49,000
[both laugh]

667
00:31:53,458 --> 00:31:54,583
[tense music playing]

668
00:31:54,666 --> 00:31:58,333
I… have to… break the news
to Olli somehow, I guess.

669
00:32:00,333 --> 00:32:01,583
You go tell him now…

670
00:32:03,333 --> 00:32:04,750
and I'll keep cleaning up.

671
00:32:11,833 --> 00:32:12,833
Ugh.

672
00:32:18,250 --> 00:32:19,166
Ugh…

673
00:32:22,625 --> 00:32:25,458
[tense, suspenseful music playing]

674
00:32:38,208 --> 00:32:39,416
Pfft.

675
00:32:40,625 --> 00:32:42,958
I spoke to Becky about our little problem.

676
00:32:43,041 --> 00:32:44,791
Mm. Excellent.

677
00:32:45,458 --> 00:32:47,791
She can see us
living in an apartment of our own.

678
00:32:47,875 --> 00:32:52,125
[scoffs] Oh please, you've already got me.
Tough luck for her.

679
00:32:53,541 --> 00:32:54,500
Tryna tell me something?

680
00:32:55,916 --> 00:32:57,750
We're looking for our own place.

681
00:32:57,833 --> 00:33:00,041
Dammit, Becky's just like Antje!

682
00:33:00,125 --> 00:33:01,166
That's ridiculous.

683
00:33:01,250 --> 00:33:03,333
Did you already forget?

684
00:33:03,416 --> 00:33:05,791
You weren't allowed
to sleep in your own bed last night.

685
00:33:05,875 --> 00:33:08,958
Once you live together, she'll start
carrying your nuts in her purse.

686
00:33:09,041 --> 00:33:10,833
You think she'll
let our trip go forward, huh?

687
00:33:10,916 --> 00:33:12,500
Yeah, of course!

688
00:33:12,583 --> 00:33:14,583
I wouldn't be so sure, my friend.

689
00:33:14,666 --> 00:33:17,333
If you give a woman your pinky,
she'll come back for the whole man.

690
00:33:17,416 --> 00:33:19,708
That's how it was with Schmitti
and all our other friends.

691
00:33:19,791 --> 00:33:22,250
At first, they're smitten
with their new woman.

692
00:33:22,333 --> 00:33:23,625
But now none of them have a spine

693
00:33:23,708 --> 00:33:26,416
or any say in the most important parts
of their lives.

694
00:33:26,500 --> 00:33:28,750
[humorous, intriguing music playing]

695
00:33:31,208 --> 00:33:32,833
She destroyed Dookie Quartet, Olli.

696
00:33:33,875 --> 00:33:35,125
What?

697
00:33:40,083 --> 00:33:40,916
Even the pretzel?

698
00:33:45,666 --> 00:33:47,625
You'll never get
that shape again, brother!

699
00:33:58,958 --> 00:34:00,458
Off to do some yoga.

700
00:34:03,541 --> 00:34:06,458
Eh… So? You tell him about our talk?

701
00:34:08,375 --> 00:34:09,500
[Rebecca] What did he say?

702
00:34:11,291 --> 00:34:13,833
[Matze] So, I thought it over.

703
00:34:13,916 --> 00:34:16,041
For me, the trip around the world
comes first.

704
00:34:16,125 --> 00:34:18,208
And, uh… we'll move in together
when I get back.

705
00:34:18,291 --> 00:34:20,083
There's no rush.

706
00:34:20,166 --> 00:34:23,500
And I'll help you
with the Moni and Hauke situation.

707
00:34:23,583 --> 00:34:25,291
I'm sure we can find you a nice apartment.

708
00:34:28,333 --> 00:34:31,208
You're right.
Maybe this isn't the best time.

709
00:34:33,333 --> 00:34:34,916
I'll be at the gym, okay?

710
00:34:37,375 --> 00:34:42,833
[woman] We align our spines by stretching
the crown of our head towards the sky.

711
00:34:42,916 --> 00:34:47,291
Drop those shoulders,
let them rise away from the ears.

712
00:34:47,375 --> 00:34:50,083
Becky, this isn't a negative thing.

713
00:34:50,166 --> 00:34:53,791
Wanting to wait a bit longer doesn't mean
he's not the right guy, you know that.

714
00:34:53,875 --> 00:34:55,333
He's in love with you, Becky.

715
00:34:55,416 --> 00:34:58,166
Why is he going along
with being manipulated?

716
00:34:58,250 --> 00:34:59,375
His heart is too big.

717
00:34:59,458 --> 00:35:01,125
Yeah, but that's what makes you like him.

718
00:35:01,791 --> 00:35:04,541
Sure, but then he should
treat me like that, not that moron Olli.

719
00:35:04,625 --> 00:35:06,416
…faces into a nice little grin.

720
00:35:07,500 --> 00:35:11,500
It's like, finally I end up with a guy
that… thinks like I do, ya know?

721
00:35:11,583 --> 00:35:16,250
I can be funny and enjoy myself
and… and be authentic.

